<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#matchlock">Glossary</a> · Term · weapon</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matchlock</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The matchlock was an early firearm mechanism in which a slow-burning match was lowered onto the priming powder to fire the gun. Matchlock <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a> spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a> in the sixteenth century. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>, musketeers armed with matchlocks fought alongside cavalry and artillery.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matchlock.jpg" alt="Matchlock" width="320" height="168" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Archiv WTS-Koblenz · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luntenschlossgewehr_System_rechts_in_der_WTS-Koblenz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>A matchlock, or firelock, is a firearm in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> is ignited by a burning piece of flammable cord or twine, brought into contact with the powder by a mechanism worked with a lever or trigger. It improved on the hand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>, which had no trigger and needed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musketeer</a> or an assistant to apply a match to the powder by hand. The earliest matchlock <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arquebus">arquebus</a> appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> by 1411 and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> by 1425, but these early guns did not have the mechanism later associated with the weapon, and the exact dating of the matchlock is disputed.</p><p>References to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissary</a> tüfek date from 1394 to 1465, and Godfrey Goodwin dates their matchlock <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arquebus">arquebuses</a> to no earlier than 1465. The first dated illustration of the mechanism is from 1475, and by the sixteenth century matchlocks were in universal use.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The classic matchlock held a slow match in a clamp at the end of a curved lever called the serpentine. Pulling a lever or, in later models, a trigger dropped the match into the flash pan, and the flash passed through the touch hole to the main charge in the barrel. Both ends of the match were usually kept alight.</p><p>Its weakness was the need to keep the match lit: damp cord was hard to light in wet weather, a glowing match gave away the carrier's position at night, and burning matches were dangerous near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a>. Keeping both ends lit every night for a year needed a mile of match. Soldiers guarding ammunition were among the first to receive self-igniting guns such as the wheellock and snaphance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> brought improved versions of the Ottoman arquebus to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1526. The Chinese obtained matchlock arquebus technology from the Portuguese in the sixteenth century, though Turkish muskets may have reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> earlier, and used matchlocks into the nineteenth century. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> the matchlock, known as the tanegashima, was first documented in 1543, when the lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanegashima">Tanegashima</a> island, Tanegashima Tokitaka, bought two from the Portuguese and had a swordsmith copy them. Its low cost and simplicity kept it in European armies until about 1750, when the flintlock replaced it. In Japan and China matchlocks saw military use to the mid-nineteenth century, and Arabian Bedouin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> kept using them into the twentieth.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Matchlock” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The change came when Ahmad adopted <b>matchlock</b> firearms and, later, cannon obtained through Arabia and the Ottomans, and organised his army to use them well.</blockquote><a href="/empire/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Armies fought mainly as infantry armed with bows, swords, spears and, from the sixteenth century, <b>matchlocks</b> and cannon, and they made skilful use of earthen ramparts, stockades and the dense forests and rivers of the valley.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ahom-kingdom">Ahom kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Ibrahim Lodi's much larger army, reportedly some 100,000 strong, was destroyed there by Babur's combination of field guns and <b>matchlocks</b> behind a barrier of linked carts and the flanking tulughma manoeuvre.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dzungar army was a mounted force of nomad archers, but from the late seventeenth century it also used <b>matchlocks</b> and, unusually for a steppe power, artillery.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Kazakh armies were tribal cavalry led by sultans and by warriors of high reputation (batyr), fighting with bows, lances and sabres and, from the seventeenth century, increasingly with <b>matchlocks</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kazakh-khanate">Kazakh Khanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Babur’s early successes owed much to <b>matchlock</b> firearms and artillery, which his successors developed further.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
